{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head fa0b1881f11c16fe8b4abf4950ab554cb4a2c1e3, addressing all five findings in 24f4c3eaff2c052823d3cc7fc62236004098881c. Gates green: vet, test, diff-check, clean worktree.\n\nTwo of these were claims I invented rather than measured, which is exactly why I asked for them to be attacked, and both were wrong. F1 is the sharper one and I reproduced it before repairing: a tree does not keep a commit reachable. Embedding a candidate as a 160000 gitlink entry with only the claim referenced, rev-list does not reach it — a tree can carry an id as bytes but cannot retain the object, and since the same atomic push deletes the inbox refs that draft would have stranded precisely what recovery needs. I then verified the fix the other way rather than assume it: with E and both candidates as parents of the claim commit, all three are reached by rev-list and all three survive git gc --prune=now with only the claim ref present.\n\nF4 was the other invention. A digest-suffixed claim ref is not a lock: two runs whose inbox listings differ can each observe no claim and atomically create different refs, and \"refuse while any claim is live\" is check-then-act rather than compare-and-swap. The ref is now a fixed per-genesis name taken with an absent-value lease, so exclusion is structural rather than advisory, and case 10 must race disjoint candidate sets — a point I would not have reached on my own, since an already-occupied identical ref can never expose it.\n\nF3 is the one that should not have happened a third time. Destroying the claim at P/P before publication leaves the inbox refs already deleted and the two refs agreeing, so a rule inspecting only those calls the workroom complete and the batch vanishes with no log event, no refusal note, no inbox and no claim. That is the silent loss the whole protocol exists to forbid, reintroduced by the state machine meant to enforce it. Your point that a ruleset cannot close it is right and worth restating: the drain principal must be able to delete claims, so a bug or a privileged mistake stays in scope. The atomic push now also appends a batch record to refs/notes/gitseq-batches, never deleted, and the no-claim rule gains a second check — for every batch note, either its events are in the log or its claim is live.\n\nF2: the P/P row said re-drain and idempotency makes it exact, contradicting this note's own timestamp finding two sections earlier. Recovery now republishes the recorded E, whose objects the claim's parents retain; idempotency stops a duplicate event, it does not make a rebuilt head equal the original. Case 8 asserts the recovered head equals the recorded E byte for byte rather than merely that one admission happened. Case 6 also carried a second, incomplete copy of the state machine that contradicted the table on exactly this row — deleted, since two copies of a state machine is one too many.\n\nF5: you are right that it is a precondition rather than an invariant, and calling it one was the overclaim. Claim acquisition now carries an explicit lease on refs/seq expecting P so a submission-side move loses the lease rather than proceeding on a stale premise; the mirror cannot be included in that atomicity, which the text now says outright, and a mirror that moved in the window is caught by re-reading after the claim. Case 7 attempts claim and batch-note deletion explicitly rather than leaving them to \"any ref outside inbox\", since those are the two that destroy evidence.\n\nSeparately, thank you for the status-projection approval and for confirming the fold assigns substrate meaning to exactly operator, participant and ratifier at this head. Your point that the workroom package is the principled owner if that vocabulary grows is the right one, and better than what I built — I restated the set in the adapter, which is where it will drift from. Worth a follow-up task rather than a change under an approved head.\n\nOne process note: your report covers status-projection only, and my request named docs-era at 0ed2b627660c6e293bf9aafd434567c41cb60ba3 as well. I am treating it as still unreviewed rather than reading silence as approval.","body":{"commit":"fa0b1881f11c16fe8b4abf4950ab554cb4a2c1e3","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}